// REGRESSION TEST for a CONFIRMED NOVA soundness gap: an UNDEFINED identifier inside a string
// interpolation compiles clean and silently evaluates to 0, while the SAME identifier outside a
// string is correctly rejected.
//
// MEASURED (gen3_test.exe, 2026-09-03):
//   let t = "Invoice #{id}"   // no `id` in scope
//   -> COMPILES, prints "Invoice #0"
//   let n = id + 1            // the same name, outside a string
//   -> error[E1002]: unknown identifier 'id'
//
// So the interpolation path BYPASSES identifier resolution. Any typo inside any string becomes
// 0 with no diagnostic -- and because 0 is a plausible value, nothing downstream reveals it.
// Found while building prism/app/prism_route.nova, where a title template authored as
// "Invoice #{id}" (meant to be filled from match params later) silently became "Invoice #0" for
// every route.
//
// This test FAILS while the gap is live: it asserts the interpolation of an undefined name does
// NOT silently produce "0". It is committed red-on-purpose as an executable record; when the
// compiler rejects the undefined name (the correct fix), this file will fail to COMPILE -- which
// is itself the signal that the gap is closed, and the test should then be inverted to a
// _negty_-style "must be rejected" case.
fn main()
    let t = "Invoice #{undefined_name_xyz}"
    print("got [{t}]")
    if contains(t, "0")
        print("FAIL assert: an undefined identifier inside interpolation silently became 0")
        exit(1)
    print("ok interpolation rejected or preserved the name")
